ts still leave space for improvements: for\ninstance, the exponents αn and βn (τ ) depend on the dimension, and it\nlooks very plausible to us that they are both non-sharp. An important\nquestion in this direction would be to improve our exponents and, if pos-\nsible, understand what the sharp exponents should be. Notice that this\n125 Quantitative stability results for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStability of Brunn-Minkowski inequalities and Minkowski type problems for nonlinear capacity | University of Essex (https://www.essex.ac.uk/research-projects/stability-of-brunn-minkowski-inequalities)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Stability of Brunn-Minkowski inequalities and Minkowski type problems for nonlinear capacity ... A mathematical operation called Minkowski addition of sets appears in convex analysis.It is defined by addition of all possible sums in the sets and it appears in motion planning, 3D solid modeling, aggregation theory, and collision detection. ... Recently, the PI and his collaborators observed that nonlinear capacity satisfies a Brunn-Minkowski type inequality and it states that a certain power of it is a concave function under the Minkowski addition of any convex compact sets including low-dimensional sets. ... This is a new and challenging direction of research as this problem has not been addressed even for the Logarithmic or Newtonian capacity associated to Laplacian.\nInspired by the recent development on stability of the classical Brunn-Minkowski inequality by M. Christ, A. Figalli, and D. Jerison, the first part of this project is devoted to studying the stability of Brunn-Minkowski inequality for nonlinear capacity associated to A-harmonic PDEs for convex compact sets. This is a new and challenging direction of research as this problem has not been addressed even for the Logarithmic or Newtonian capacity associated to Laplacian. The project will also investigate sharpness of these inequalities for non-convex sets.\n\nOnce the Brunn-Minkowski inequality has been studied, it is natural to study a related problem which is known as the Minkowski problem. This problem consists in finding a convex polyhedron from data consisting of normals to their faces and their surface areas. In the smooth case, the corresponding problem for convex bodies is to find the convex body given the Gauss curvature of its boundary, as a function of the unit normal. The proof consists of three parts: existence, uniqueness, and regularity.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/413548394_Logarithmic_Brunn--Minkowski_Inequality_under_n-2_Reflection_Symmetries)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries  * August 2026 ...   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet. ... We prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies with symmetries to n-2 orthogonal hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the related logarithmic Minkowski problem. ... Minkowski problem in a systematic theory [18,19]. ... arXiv:2608.20730v1 [math.MG] 21 Aug 2026 ... n-reection theorem of Böröczky and Kalantzopoulos already applies [3, Theorem 2]. ... clidean norm by |·|, the n-dimensional volume by V(·), the 2-dimensional volume/area\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries\n\n  * August 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2608.20730\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nXiaoRui Lu\n\nXiaoRui Lu\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies with symmetries to n-2 orthogonal hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the related logarithmic Minkowski problem. We also generalize the inequality from the Lebesgue measure to certain log-concave measure.\n\nLOGARITHMIC BRUNN–MINKOWSKI INEQUALITY\n\nUNDER n−2 REFLECTION SYMMETRIES\n\nXIAORUI LU\n\nAbstract. We prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex\n\nbodies with symmetries to n-2 orthogonal hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case\n\nand the uniqueness of the related logarithmic Minkowski problem. We also generalize\n\nthe inequality from the Lebesgue measure to certain log-concave measure.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nThe classical Brunn-Minkowski inequality is one of the fundamental principles of con-\n\nvex geometry. If K, L ⊂R n are convex bodies and 0≤λ≤1, then\n\n(1.1) V(1 −λ)K+λL1\n\nn≥(1 −λ)V(K)1\n\nn+λV (L)1\n\nn,\n\nwith equality if and only if K and L are homothetic for 0<λ<1. It has become\n\na starting point for several extensions of the classical Minkowski theory; see [27] for\n\nbackground.\n\nIn the 1960s, Firey [10] generalized the Minkowski combination of convex bodies to\n\nthe L p-Minkowski combination (1−λ)·K+p λ·L. When p≥1, for convex bodies K and\n\nL containing the origin in their interiors, it is dened as the convex body with support\n\nfunction h(1−λ)·K+p λ·L:= ((1 −λ)h p\n\nK+λh p\n\nL)1\n\np. The associated Brunn–Minkowski–Firey\n\ninequality reads as follows for p≥1,\n\n(1.2) V(1 −λ)·K+p λ·Lp\n\nn≥(1 −λ)V(K)p\n\nn+λV (L)p\n\nn,\n\nwhich has an equivalent form\n\n(1.3) V(1 −λ)·K+p λ·L≥V(K)1−λ V(L)λ,\n\nLutwak’s work in the 1990s placed this inequality, the L p mixed volumes, and the L p-\n\nMinkowski problem in a systematic theory [18,19].\n\nHowever, when p∈(0,1),((1 −λ)h p\n\nK+λh p\n\nL)1\n\np may not be a support function for\n\nany convex body in general. Böröczky, Lutwak, Yang and Zhang [4] found a natural\n\nKey words and phrases. convex body, reection symmetry, log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality.\n\n1\n\narXiv:2608.20730v1 [math.MG] 21 Aug 2026\n\n2 XIAORUI LU\n\ngeneralization of L p-Minkowski sum as follows\n\n(1 −λ)·K+p λ·L:= \\\n\nx∈S n−1\n\n{z∈R n:x·z≤((1 −λ)h p\n\nK(x) + λh p\n\nL(x))1\n\np}.\n\nIt is natural to further dene the log-Minkowski combination (L 0-sum), (1−λ)·K+0 λ·L,\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThin shell implies spectral gap up to polylog via a stochastic localization scheme (https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.0893)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.6 years ago; Title: Thin shell implies spectral gap up to polylog via a stochastic localization scheme ... We establish quantitative connections between two well-known open problems related to this inequality, namely, the thin shell conjecture, and the conjecture by Kannan, Lovasz, and Simonovits, showing that the corresponding optimal bounds are equivalent up to logarithmic factors.\nTitle: Thin shell implies spectral gap up to polylog via a stochastic localization scheme\nAuthors: Ronen Eldan\nDate: Mon Mar  5 12:54:51 2012\n\nWe consider the isoperimetric inequality on the class of high-dimensional isotropic convex bodies. We establish quantitative connections between two well-known open problems related to this inequality, namely, the thin shell conjecture, and the conjecture by Kannan, Lovasz, and Simonovits, showing that the corresponding optimal bounds are equivalent up to logarithmic factors. In particular we prove that, up to logarithmic factors, the minimal possible ratio between surface area and volume is attained on ellipsoids. We also show that a positive answer to the thin shell conjecture would imply an optimal dependence on the dimension in a certain formulation of the Brunn-Minkowski inequality. Our results rely on the construction of a stochastic localization scheme for log-concave measures.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Logarithmic Brunn-Minkowski and the Logarithmic Minkowski conjectures 329 (https://www.renyi.hu/~carlos/Book_Brunn_Minkowski-2025-09-07.pdf)\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; The Logarithmic Brunn-Minkowski and the Logarithmic Minkowski conjectures 329 ... For unconditional convex bodies, Conjectures 8.7.1 and 8.7.3 have been verified by Saroglou [633] (see Section 8.7.6).This result was extended to convex bodies invariant under reflections through n independent linear hyperplanes by Böröczky, Kalantzopoulos [133], and even a stability version is provided by Böröczky, De [121] in this case.\nThe Logarithmic Brunn-Minkowski and the Logarithmic Minkowski conjectures 329\n\n*Log Brunn Minkowski in terms of optimal transport due to Kolesnikov [466]:* The paper [466] provides an equivalent formulation of the Log Brunn-Minkowski Conjecture for o-symmetric convex bodies in terms of the displacement convexity of certain functional of probability measures on the sphere.\n\n**8.7.2 Some known cases of the Log Brunn-Minkowski and the Log Minkowski conjectures**\n\nThe Log Brunn-Minkowski Conjecture 8.7.1 and the Log Minkowski Conjecture 8.7.3 are still open but have been verified in various cases. In ℝ², Conjectures 8.7.1 and 8.7.3 are verified by Böröczky, Lutwak, Yang, Zhang [136] for origin symmetric convex bodies, but it is still open for general centered planar convex bodies.\n\nFor unconditional convex bodies, Conjectures 8.7.1 and 8.7.3 have been verified by Saroglou [633] (see Section 8.7.6). This result was extended to convex bodies invariant under reflections through n independent linear hyperplanes by Böröczky, Kalantzopoulos [133], and even a stability version is provided by Böröczky, De [121] in this case. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLatest